"00_library_aliases.mst — the library global names the Dolphin corpus writes (DD9).

 HAND-WRITTEN, and it must load AFTER the generated libraries — hence its own
 layer rather than a file in `rt/`, which loads BEFORE them. Measured: with the
 aliases in `rt/` every global bound nil, because `UserLibrary` did not exist
 yet when the assignments ran. The layer stack is therefore
 `st/world;st/dolphin_compat;st/prims/rt;st/prims/structs;st/prims;st/prims/aliases`.

 Dolphin's MVP code does NOT say `UserLibrary default beginPaint: …`. It says
 `User32 beginPaint: …` — a bare global naming the module. Censused over the
 whole corpus rather than guessed:

     User32   434    OleAut32  53    ComCtl32  22
     Kernel32 209    Ole32     44    Shell32   21
     Gdi32    148    AdvApi32  36    ComDlg32  13
                     UxTheme   26    Shlwapi   13
                                     Version    2

 versus `XxxLibrary default …` at **10 sites in all of MVP**. So the alias is
 the idiom that matters, and translated code binds through these names.

 They are GLOBALS, not classes: the loader puts a global on STGlobals and class
 names win reads, so a name that is not a class (no class is called `User32`)
 resolves to the global. Assigning the class object itself means `User32 foo:`
 is a class-side send to the generated library — exactly what the generated
 methods are.

 A module the corpus names but this build cannot resolve (ICU, WebView2,
 Scintilla — see st/prims/README.md) is deliberately absent here: binding it
 would turn a clear `unresolved symbol` at the call into a confusing
 doesNotUnderstand on a nil global."

User32   := UserLibrary.
Kernel32 := KernelLibrary.
Gdi32    := GDILibrary.
OleAut32 := OLEAutLibrary.
Ole32    := OLELibrary.
AdvApi32 := AdvApiLibrary.
UxTheme  := ThemeLibrary.
ComCtl32 := CommCtrlLibrary.
Shell32  := ShellLibrary.
ComDlg32 := ComDlgLibrary.
Shlwapi  := ShlwapiLibrary.
Version  := VersionLibrary.

"── the four that were MISSING, and how they were found ─────────────────────

 The table above was assembled from a census of call sites, which finds the
 names the corpus uses OFTEN and silently omits the ones it uses once. Each
 omission is an unbound global — nil — and a send to nil inside a contained
 handler, which is the hardest failure in this port to trace back to its
 cause. `SHCore` cost a sitting: `DisplayMonitor>>getDpi:` is
 `SHCore getDpiForMonitor: handle ...`, reached from `effectiveDpi`, which is
 the first statement of `cacheInfo` — so no monitor could report its
 rectangle, and every window-positioning path above it failed, `showModal`
 included.

 So this half is not a census. Dolphin states the mapping itself: every
 library class answers `sharedVariableName` with the global it binds to
 (`OS.SHCoreLibrary` answers `#{SHCore}`), and these four are the ones where
 that declaration names a global this port had not bound and the translated
 wave actually sends to:

     VM       17 sends    Kernel.VMLibrary
     Dwmapi    2 sends    OS.DwmApiLibrary
     Ucrt      1 send     OS.CRTLibrary
     SHCore    1 send     OS.SHCoreLibrary

 The remaining declared globals (BCrypt, Crypt32, Gdip, ICU, Odbc32, PathCch,
 Rpcrt4, Ws2_32) have zero sends in this wave and stay unbound, for the reason
 the header gives: an unresolvable module is better as a loud failure at the
 call than as a nil global."
SHCore   := SHCoreLibrary.
Dwmapi   := DwmApiLibrary.
Ucrt     := CRTLibrary.
VM       := VMLibrary.

"── NtDll, and the Wine check (DD10) ────────────────────────────────────────

 `SystemMetrics class >> cacheDpiIndependentSettings` is `IsNotWine := NtDll
 isWine not`, and `hasTextBoxMargins` answers `IsNotWine` — which `TextEdit`
 asks on the create path, because Wine did not implement EM_SETMARGINS.

 `NtDll` is the corpus's global for the NT library; `NTLibrary` is what
 genprims generated it as, so this is the same alias the table above makes for
 User32 and its kin."
NtDll := NTLibrary.

"Dolphin's own test, and a REAL one rather than a hardcoded false: Wine
 exports `wine_get_version` from ntdll, and nothing else does. Answering a
 constant would be right on this machine and wrong the moment the port is run
 under Wine — which is a plausible thing to do with a Windows Smalltalk, and
 exactly the kind of assumption that is invisible until it is wrong."
Object subclass: NTLibrary [
    NTLibrary class >> isWine [
        | mod h name found |
        "GetModuleHandleW takes a WIDE string; the wrapper's `FFICoerce
         pointer:` refuses a Smalltalk String outright (the floor doing its
         job), so marshal it here."
        mod := Utf16Buffer fromString: 'ntdll.dll'.
        h := [ Kernel32 getModuleHandle: mod ] ensure: [ mod free ].
        (h isNil or: [ h = 0 ]) ifTrue: [ ^false ].
        "GetProcAddress has no W variant: the symbol name is ALWAYS ANSI, so
         this one needs a byte buffer rather than a Utf16Buffer. A wide string
         here would look like 'w' followed by a NUL and find nothing."
        name := self ansiBuffer: 'wine_get_version'.
        found := [ Kernel32 getProcAddress: h lpProcName: name ]
            ensure: [ name free ].
        ^found notNil and: [ found ~= 0 ]
    ]

    "A NUL-terminated ANSI (single-byte) copy of a String, for the handful of
     APIs that have no wide variant."
    NTLibrary class >> ansiBuffer: aString [
        | n buf |
        n := aString size.
        buf := ExternalMemory new: n + 1.
        1 to: n do: [ :i | buf byteAt: i put: (aString at: i) value ].
        buf byteAt: n + 1 put: 0.
        ^buf
    ]
]

"── GetMenu — a hand-written prim, and why the floor does not have it ───────

 `st/prims` is GENERATED from Dolphin's own `<stdcall:>` pragmas, so it holds
 exactly what Dolphin declared. Dolphin never declares GetMenu: it keeps the
 menu bar in its `ShellView` and never asks Windows for it back, so the
 pragma does not exist to generate from.

 This port wants it for one reason — a GATE that asks WINDOWS whether the
 window really has the menu, rather than asking the Smalltalk object that
 was told to attach it. A menu built and never attached satisfies every
 other assertion.

 Declared HERE rather than hand-edited into `st/prims`, which is a build
 artifact. The alias layer is the right home: it already loads after the
 generated libraries and already exists to add what the corpus idiom needs on
 top of them."
Object subclass: UserLibrary [
    UserLibrary class >> getMenu: hWnd [
        <primitive: FFI function: #GetMenu ret: #h args: #( g )>
    ]
]
